From 174dd45d2c888b23d26360ca98b78083a5aeb62b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 10:01:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(videos): treat Property Saraansh Reviews as a second
 source channel
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The brand now publishes from two YouTube channels: the main channel for
market analysis and long-form comparisons, and @PropertySaraanshReviews
(UChOfCm-Xo4NABiBfKuw7H0g) for the project-by-project reviews people
search by project name.

Both are the same business, so the site treats them as one portfolio:

  - src/lib/channels.ts is the single registry — ids, names, handle URLs,
    and the helpers that answer "is this one of ours" and "which channel
    published this". Ids stay overridable per environment.
  - /our-videos pulls newest uploads from every registered channel in
    parallel and merges them newest-first, so a project review sits in the
    same feed as a market update, in date order. One channel failing can
    no longer empty the other, and duplicate ids are collapsed.
  - A watch page is served for a new upload from either channel, not just
    the main one. Previously getLiveVideoById rejected anything whose
    channelId did not match the single hardcoded channel, which would have
    404'd every Reviews upload until someone hand-wrote an entry for it.
  - Both channel URLs are listed in the Organization sameAs, which is what
    tells Google the two channels and this site are one entity rather than
    three strangers, and both appear in the footer.
  - The watch page names the channel a video came from and offers that
    channel's subscribe link, so someone who came for a project review can
    follow the channel that actually publishes project reviews.

Videos carry a channelId through hydration. Curated entries predate the
second channel and have none, so they resolve to the main channel.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
